\image(figures/seidel_first.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The second coordinate reads the first coordinate’s new value. After one sweep the residual is 0.75, so a zero-tolerance request has exhausted its one-sweep budget without converging.)
\alttext(Three boxes show (0,0), then (0.5,0), then (0.5,0.75). Arrows identify the two row solves and emphasize that the second reads the new first coordinate.)

> From scalar updates to a triangular solve

Let A be a real n-by-n matrix with nonzero diagonal and b a matching vector.
Write A=D+L+U with diagonal D and strictly lower/upper parts L,U.
With row indices increasing through a sweep, the exact-arithmetic update is

$$
x_i^{(k+1)}=\frac{b_i-\sum_{j<i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k+1)}-\sum_{j>i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k)}}{a_{ii}},
\qquad (D+L)x^{(k+1)}=b-Ux^{(k)}.
$$
\alttext(Each coordinate uses newly updated lower-index coordinates and old higher-index coordinates; the complete sweep solves a lower triangular system with the old upper contribution on the right.)

This is an ordered triangular operation. A generic matrix-vector callback alone
does not expose the diagonal and lower part needed by this API.
[Barrett and coauthors, Templates for the Solution of Linear Systems, second edition,
Gauss–Seidel subsection](https://netlib.org/linalg/html_templates/node14.html)
describe the immediate-update dependency and its sensitivity to ordering.
The library implements a dense scalar version: zero start, ordered long-double
sums and checked double assignments. Its recomputed stopping norm is formed from
a dense A x whose row results have first been rounded to double. All input rows,
RHS values, controls and nonzero diagonal entries are validated even when the
initial residual already meets the tolerance.

If x* solves the system, subtraction gives the error recurrence

$$
e^{(k+1)}=T e^{(k)},\qquad T=-(D+L)^{-1}U,\qquad e^{(k)}=x^{(k)}-x^*.
$$
\alttext(The next error is the Gauss-Seidel iteration matrix times the old error; the iteration matrix is minus the inverse lower triangular part times the strictly upper part.)

In exact arithmetic, convergence from every initial vector is equivalent to
spectral radius rho(T)<1: T^k then tends to zero. Eigenvalues inside the unit
circle give decaying Jordan-block powers; any eigenvalue outside or on the unit
circle prevents decay for some initial vector. This criterion does not promise
monotone Euclidean residuals or a perfectly straight logarithmic residual plot.
Transient effects, multiple modes, finite termination and rounding all matter.

Strict row diagonal dominance is sufficient. To see why, let
q=max_i sum_(j≠i)|a_ij|/|a_ii|<1 and E=||e_old||∞. In the first row the new error
is at most qE. Inductively, previously updated errors are at most qE≤E and all
remaining old errors are at most E; the next new error is therefore also at
most qE. Thus a complete sweep contracts the infinity norm by at most q.
For symmetric positive-definite A, each coordinate update instead minimizes the
strictly convex quadratic f(x)=xᵀAx/2−bᵀx along that coordinate: the exact decrease
is a_ii times the squared coordinate change divided by 2. A full nontrivial
homogeneous error sweep strictly reduces its A-norm; compactness of the A-unit
sphere supplies a contraction factor below 1. Neither proof certifies a particular
floating-point residual decrease at every sweep.

> The original three-equation system, using the solver’s actual history

The original equations are 4x−y+z=7, 4x−8y+z=−21 and −2x+y+5z=15.
Their exact solution is (2,4,3), and every row is strictly diagonally dominant.
The old scalar call used absolute tolerance 10^-10; its separate plotting loop
used 10^-12 and double-only intermediate sums. This report keeps the scalar
call’s semantics and plots the actual native recomputed residuals at 10^-10.
Those are different experiments, so the old local plotting replay is removed.
The source’s cached residual was 3.72223e−11. Running its unchanged scalar program
against the pre-conversion core gives 3.72241e−11; this conversion preserves that
executed result exactly.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/seidel_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gauss_seidel.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const std::vector<std::vector<double>> a{{4,-1,1},{4,-8,1},{-2,1,5}};const std::vector<double>b{7,-21,15};
 const auto t=dm::algorithms::gauss_seidel_history(a,b);const auto s=dm::algorithms::gauss_seidel(a,b);
 std::print("| sweep | x | y | z | actual stopping residual |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | {:.9e} |\n",t.initial_residual);
 std::vector<double>k{0},logs{std::log10(t.initial_residual)};
 for(const auto&e:t.sweeps){std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9e} |\n",e.number,e.x[0],e.x[1],e.x[2],e.residual);if(e.residual>0){k.push_back(e.number);logs.push_back(std::log10(e.residual));}}
 std::print("\nConverged {}; sweeps {}; residual {:.12e}; off-diagonal products {}; residual products {}; norm evaluations {}; legacy result identical {}.\n",s.converged,s.iterations,s.residual,t.run.work.off_diagonal_products,t.run.work.residual_products,t.run.work.residual_evaluations,s.x==t.run.solution.x&&s.residual==t.run.solution.residual);
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.lines(k,logs,"Actual native residual",{37,99,235},2).points(k,logs,"",{37,99,235},5)
  .title("Original system: actual residuals at absolute tolerance 1e-10").xlab("Completed sweeps; zero is the initial state").ylab("log10 positive recomputed Euclidean residual").size(1100,540).save_svg("build/seidel_original.svg");
 if(!s.converged||s.iterations!=13||s.x!=t.run.solution.x)throw std::runtime_error("preserved original solve");
}
