\image(figures/seidel_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Thirteen actual sweeps reproduce the original scalar result. The line joins positive measured residuals without inventing a logarithmic floor or asserting an exactly constant reduction factor.)
\alttext(A plotted native residual history starts near 27 and falls to about 3.72 times 10^-11 in 13 sweeps, with the computed coordinate values and dense product counts printed in the table.)

The stopping test is inclusive: computed residual≤tol. It is an absolute,
unscaled test, so rescaling both A and b can change when the same tolerance is
met even though the mathematical solution is unchanged. A small residual is
also not a forward-error certificate: in exact arithmetic e=−A^-1r for r=b−Ax,
so the inverse matrix can amplify a small residual. Rounding in the stored A x
adds another distinction between this implementation’s stopping norm and an
exact real-number residual. A zero computed residual is reported as zero and
has no logarithm; no plotted value is substituted for it.

> An exactly analysable family shows decay and divergence

Choose A=[[1,−q],[−q,1]], b=(1−q,1−q), q>0, q≠1. The exact solution is (1,1).
For a zero start, the error after k≥1 sweeps and the exact residual norm are

$$
e^{(k)}=(-q^{2k-1},-q^{2k}),\quad
T=\begin{pmatrix}0&q\\0&q^2\end{pmatrix},\quad
\|b-Ax^{(k)}\|_2=|q^{2k-1}(1-q^2)|.
$$
\alttext(The two error coordinates are minus successive odd and even powers of q; the iteration matrix has eigenvalues zero and q squared; the residual is the absolute odd power times one minus q squared.)

These expressions follow by substituting the first coordinate assignment into
the second. Hence rho(T)=q². Jacobi’s matrix has eigenvalues ±q and spectral
radius q. The square relation explains the commonly observed “twice the rate”
for this particular family in exact asymptotic sweep counts. It is not a universal
speed ratio, does not compare wall-clock time and says nothing about a general
matrix’s Jacobi convergence. Here q<1 gives strict diagonal dominance and decay;
q>1 makes this particular zero-start error grow. All requested eight sweeps are
kept below, so a false converged flag means the zero-tolerance budget was exhausted.
The Jacobi column is the derived exact spectral radius; no Jacobi solver is run.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/seidel_rates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
