> A residual can rise before convergence, and order changes the intermediate state

For A=[[1,1/2],[0,1/128]], b=(0,1), both rows are strictly diagonally dominant.
The exact solution is (−64,128). Starting at zero, the first row remains zero;
the second becomes 128. This makes the first equation’s residual 64. The next
sweep sets the first coordinate to −64 and reaches the solution exactly in binary
arithmetic. The sequence of Euclidean residuals is 1,64,0 despite rho(T)=0.
This is a nilpotent, nonnormal iteration: finite termination after two sweeps
coexists with a large transient residual. It directly disproves an unconditional
monotone-residual or straight-log-line claim.

The second panel returns to the symmetric two-equation example. Reverse both
the row and variable ordering, run one forward sweep, then map coordinates back.
Its first state becomes (3/4,1/2) rather than (1/2,3/4). In this symmetric example
the rate is unchanged, but the intermediate iterates differ; other matrices can
also change rate under reordering. A row-only permutation does not perform this
same transformation and may introduce a zero diagonal.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/seidel_transient.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gauss_seidel.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto t=dm::algorithms::gauss_seidel_history({{1,.5},{0,1./128}}, {0,1},10,0,0);
 std::vector<double>k{0},r{t.initial_residual};std::print("| sweep | first coordinate | second coordinate | residual |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 0 | 0 | {:.0f} |\n",t.initial_residual);
 for(const auto&e:t.sweeps){k.push_back(e.number);r.push_back(e.residual);std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",e.number,e.x[0],e.x[1],e.residual);}
 const auto f=dm::algorithms::gauss_seidel_solve({{2,-1},{-1,2}},{1,1},1,0);
 const auto reverse=dm::algorithms::gauss_seidel_solve({{2,-1},{-1,2}},{1,1},1,0);
 const std::vector<double>mapped{reverse.solution.x[1],reverse.solution.x[0]};
 std::print("\nForward one-sweep vector ({:.2f}, {:.2f}); reversed order mapped back ({:.2f}, {:.2f}); triangular example converged {} in {} sweeps.\n",f.solution.x[0],f.solution.x[1],mapped[0],mapped[1],t.run.solution.converged,t.run.solution.iterations);
 auto growth=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();growth.lines(k,r,"",{220,38,38},2).points(k,r,"",{220,38,38},6).title("Strict dominance, transient growth").xlab("Completed sweeps").ylab("Actual residual, linear scale").y_limits(0,75);
 auto order=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();order.lines({1,2},f.solution.x,"Forward",{37,99,235},2).points({1,2},f.solution.x,"",{37,99,235},6)
  .lines({1,2},mapped,"Reverse, mapped back",{220,38,38},2).points({1,2},mapped,"",{220,38,38},6).legend({"Forward","Reverse, mapped back"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
  .title("Order changes the first iterate").xlab("Original variable index").ylab("Value after one sweep").y_limits(0,1.1);
 dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(growth).add(order).size(1100,500).title("Convergence need not mean monotone residuals or identical ordered iterates").fit_panels().save_svg("build/seidel_transient.svg");
 if(t.sweeps.size()!=2||r!=std::vector<double>{1,64,0}||mapped==f.solution.x)throw std::runtime_error("transient and ordering");
}
