\caption(The scaling inputs are the native one-dimensional Laplacian matrices. Product counts include their stored zeros. The right panel’s 16-entry admission is exact for a nonconverged two-coordinate one-sweep history.)
\alttext(Two panels show quadratic off-diagonal and residual product counts through 64 variables and complete-history entry counts 16 required,16 captured and 15 rejected. Printed controls distinguish whole admission from online residual caps.)

The native histories preserve the original solver result and make each dependency
visible. Exact convergence theory explains the diagonal-dominant example and the
q-family’s decay, while the triangular counterexample shows why residual monotonicity
cannot be assumed. Ordering, absolute stopping, arithmetic precision and explicit
dense storage all shape the computation a user actually receives. These constructed
linear systems test the mathematical claims directly; no external dataset or timing
benchmark is needed.
